A face needs the registry and a transport to select it:

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment-face.toml --face constrained --link-interval 60 --link-protocol unixsock-stream
? 2
Error: --face plans from the registry's `exposure` markers (RFC 08 §2); pass --registry <dir> (or a context that names one)

$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment-face.toml --registry ../fixture-tests/registry --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --face constrained --link-interval 60
? 2
no session (failed to open the Zenoh session); answering from --registry only: ../fixture-tests/registry.
That is what this checkout declares, not what the fleet serves — `zenctl check conform <producer> --registry <dir>` compares them when the bus is reachable (RFC 05 §3.1).
Error: --face needs the transport that selects it: --link-protocol <PROTOCOL> (a unixsock-stream modem lane) or --link-interface <IFACE>

```

## Principals on the face: a `user`, and what is refused

An operator on the far side, authenticated by the face's usrpwd, is enrolled
by `user` (v1.49). Its subject is the user *on the face's transport*, and its
policy repeats every face deny: a transport matches every subject whose
properties match, the face's own included, and zenoh lets any matching
subject's allow win — so an allow beside the face's denies would open the
face. Of `deny-rpc` it repeats the legs no call uses (`deny-rpc-legs`) and
carves the rest to the console's write shape: the granted write allowed, the
other declared writes denied. A certificate principal is refused by name,
never dropped — with exit 1, because a row was refused.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment-user.toml --json5 --registry ../fixture-tests/registry --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --face constrained --link-protocol unixsock-stream --link-interval none
? 1
no session (failed to open the Zenoh session); answering from --registry only: ../fixture-tests/registry.
That is what this checkout declares, not what the fleet serves — `zenctl check conform <producer> --registry <dir>` compares them when the bus is reachable (RFC 05 §3.1).
