## `--check --against`: the plan against a router's config file

The running block is not observable: zenoh 1.10's admin space serves no GET
under `config/**` (it subscribes to that subtree for runtime edits). So the
observed side is the config *file*, read through zenoh's own loader — the
same parse `zenohd` does, so a block that would be refused at startup is
refused here. The clean fixture is the plan's own block, wrapped.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment tests/cmd/fixtures/enrollment.toml --format table --check --against tests/cmd/fixtures/router-acl.json5
no registry asked: the planes are as the enrollment claims, and no-remote-actions denies the convention's write leaf zensight/v1/*/@rpc/*/**/set rather than the declared write procedures — pass --registry <dir> to narrow both to what the fleet actually declares (RFC 13 §3 O4)
tests/cmd/fixtures/router-acl.json5: 20 rule(s) and 6 subject(s) configured; 20 and 6 planned
PASS
compared against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/router-acl.json5 through zenoh's own loader — zenoh 1.10 serves no GET on `@/<zid>/router/config/**`, so the running block is not observable from the bus and a file that differs from what the router was started with is invisible here (RFC 13 §3 O5)
interest propagation not asked: whether a consumer's declared interest reaches the publishers' faces is observable only from a publisher's matching listener, which a config-file check has no way to hold (RFC 13 §3 O4)

```

The drifted one: disabled, the shared `interest-prop` dropped (the fifth
fact's failure), one host's serve rule made ingress-only (a host that
answers no query — fact 4), and a CN the enrollment never enrolled.

```console
